  "steps": [
    {
      "type": "learn",
      "title": "Data plus the rules that protect it",
      "body": [
        "A tuple is only data: anything can change `Copies` to -4 and nothing objects. Libraries have rules (*you cannot lend a copy that is not on the shelf*), and the best place for a rule is **next to the data it protects**. That is what a class is for:",
        "",
        "```csharp",
        "class Book",
        "{",
        "    public Book(string isbn, string title, int copies)",
        "    {",
        "        Isbn = isbn;",
        "        Title = title;",
        "        TotalCopies = copies;",
        "        AvailableCopies = copies;",
        "    }",
        "",
        "    public string Isbn { get; }                       // set once, in the constructor",
        "    public string Title { get; }",
        "    public int TotalCopies { get; }",
        "    public int AvailableCopies { get; private set; }  // only Book can change it",
        "",
        "    public bool TryLend()",
        "    {",
        "        if (AvailableCopies == 0) return false;",
        "        AvailableCopies--;",
        "        return true;",
        "    }",
        "}",
        "```",
        "",
        "- The **constructor** runs once when you write `new Book(...)` and sets the object up in a valid state.",
        "- `{ get; }` properties are read-only after construction; `private set` means *readable by everyone, writable only by me*.",
        "- `TryLend` is the **only** way to take a copy, so the rule cannot be bypassed. This is **encapsulation**.",
        "",
        "The same idea applies to the catalog itself: a `Catalog` class owns the list and the ISBN index as **private** fields, and offers `Add`, `Find` and `All`. Nobody outside can put a book in the list without going through the duplicate check.",
        "",
        "Classes go at the **bottom** of the file, after all the top-level code and local functions."
      ]
    },
    {
